# SYNOPSIS

```usage
ls [-dlmnpqrstuFQT] [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

For each directory argument, `ls` lists the contents of the directory; for each other <file>, it repeats its name.
With no <file>, it lists the current directory.
Names are sorted as bytes, each directory's contents on their own, and a name with a space or one of rc's special characters is quoted as rc would quote it.
When more than one <file> is given, each name is shown with its path.

: `-d`
  If an argument is a directory, list it, not its contents.
: `-l`
  List in long format: the mode, the device, the owner and group, the size in bytes and the time the file was last changed.
: `-m`
  Print the name of the user who last changed the file before each name.
: `-n`
  Don't sort.
: `-p`
  Print only the last element of each path.
: `-q`
  Print each file's qid, its path, version and type, before its name.
: `-r`
  Reverse the order of the sort.
: `-s`
  Print each file's size in kilobytes before its name.
: `-t`
  Sort by the time the file was last changed, newest first.
: `-u`
  Accepted, for the time of the last access: it shows the time of the last change.
: `-F`
  Add `/` after each directory's name.
: `-Q`
  Don't quote names.
: `-T`
  Print `-` before each name, for a file that isn't temporary.

A time within the last six months is shown as the month, day and time of day; an older one, or one in the future, as the month, day and year.

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% ls -F /svc
```

